

{"id":"4e072523-8b31-42fa-83c6-bad781eec5f2","object":"chat.completion","created":1786450732,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_071\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 24,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_11\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 20,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 3,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 14,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_07\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 4,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated repair optimization. The task is to produce a repair that respects all constraints and overrides. The baseline provided is a candidate solution that we might need to modify to fix all issues (e.g., overrides that cause conflicts). We have a zero-shot repair task: we need to return one complete repair covering every mission exactly once, respecting fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. The lexicographic optimization order is: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. We have to output decisions for each mission.\n\nFirst, understand the instance: there are 7 missions assigned to 3 UAVs. The assignment is fixed: MISSION_01 and MISSION_05 are assigned to UAV_02; MISSION_02 and MISSION_04 assigned to UAV_01; MISSION_03, MISSION_06, MISSION_07 assigned to UAV_03. Note the fixed UAV order: each UAV has a sequence_index: MISSION_01 seq 1 (UAV_02), MISSION_05 seq 0 (UAV_02) → so order for UAV_02: first MISSION_05 (seq 0), then MISSION_01 (seq 1). UAV_01: MISSION_04 seq 0, MISSION_02 seq 1. UAV_03: MISSION_07 seq 0, MISSION_03 seq 1, MISSION_06 seq 2. So the order for each UAV is fixed by sequence_index.\n\nWe have to consider the overrides from the intervention:\n- Initial SOC overrides: UAV_02 initial_soc_kwh set to 1.659556 (down from baseline 2.750553). So UAV_02 starts with less energy.\n- Pad capacity overrides: VP_02 capacity 1 at slot 5. So at slot 5, only one pad available at VP_02.\n- Closed corridor slots: COR_02 is closed from slots 6 to 29 (inclusive). COR_08 is closed from slots 3 to 26 (inclusive). So any path using those corridors cannot depart during those slots (i.e., the corridor must be open for the entire travel). Travel time: single slot offset, so departure slot + travel slots -1 must be within open slots? Actually, edge segments have offset_slot 0, so departure slot must be such that the corridor is open at that slot. So if a corridor is closed at slot s, you cannot be in that corridor at that slot. Since travel slots indicate duration, if a path uses COR_02 with travel slots 2 (PATH_08), it requires slots departure and departure+1 to be open. Similarly, COR_08 is used by PATH_10 (travel slots 2). So those paths are unavailable if the corridor is closed. PATH_04 uses COR_04 (open all slots?), PATH_08 uses COR_02, PATH_11 uses COR_10. PATH_05 uses COR_03. PATH_07 uses COR_07. PATH_09 uses COR_05. PATH_06 uses COR_01. PATH_02 uses COR_11 (travel slots 2), PATH_03 uses COR_06 (travel slots 3), PATH_10 uses COR_08 (travel slots 2), PATH_01 uses COR_09 (travel slots 2). Check closures: COR_02 closed 6-29, so PATH_08 cannot depart at slots where departure or departure+1 falls in that range. Since travel slots 2, departure slot must be such that both departure and departure+1 are not in closed range. That means departure < 6 or departure+1 > 29? Actually, closed means you cannot use it at all during closure. So departure must be <=5 (then departure+1=6 might be closed? Actually slot 6 is closed. If departure=5, travel slots 2: occupies slots 5 and 6. Slot 5 is open? Corridor closures: COR_02 closed slots 6-29, so slot 5 is open. So departure=5 is allowed because only slot 5 is open and slot 6 is closed? The corridor is closed at slot 6, so you cannot be in that corridor at slot 6. Therefore, a flight that requires slot 6 is invalid. So departure must be such that all occupied slots are open. For travel slots 2, departure+1 must be <=5 or departure >29? departure=5 gives slot 5 (open), slot 6 (closed) → invalid. So departure must be <=4 (then slot 4 and 5? wait, slot 4 is before closure? closure starts at 6, so 4-5 are open. So departure=4 yields slots 4 and 5, both open. departure=5 yields slots 5 and 6, 6 closed. So max departure for PATH_08 is 4. Alternatively, departure >=30? But horizon slots max 32, departure must be after closure, but that would be very late. So likely PATH_08 is only usable in early slots. Similarly COR_08 closed 3-26. PATH_10 uses COR_08, travel slots 2. So departure must be such that both slots are open: departure must be such that departure and departure+1 not in 3-26. That means departure <=1 (since 1,2 open; 2,3? slot 3 closed, so departure=2 gives slots 2,3 invalid) or departure >=27? departure=27 gives slots 27,28? Slot 27 is closed? COR_08 closed up to 26. So slot 27 is open. departure=27 gives 27,28 both open. But note departure slot 27 might be beyond due dates. So PATH_10 may be only in first couple slots or very late.\n\nOther closures: none for other corridors.\n\nPad capacity: VP_02 has capacity 1 at slot 5. So only one UAV can be at VP_02 at that slot for takeoff/landing/turnaround? Pad capacity applies to presence at vertiport during slots. Possibly for landing and departure slots. We need to schedule missions such that at any slot, the number of UAVs at a vertiport does not exceed pad capacity. Since UAVs may have turnaround after service flight (1 slot turnaround at destination). Also, the post-service charge occurs at destination after turnaround. So occupancy timing: a mission arrives at destination at departure_slot + travel_slots. Then turnaround of 1 slot (occupying pad during that slot?), then post-service charge. The UAV stays at destination until next mission departure? Actually, after turnaround, the UAV is available for next mission but may be charging. The pad capacity is only relevant during the mission's arrival and turnaround? Typically, pad capacity at vertiport limits simultaneous takeoffs and landings. Usually, a UAV occupies a pad from start of arrival slot through departure of next mission? In integrated repair tasks, pad capacity constraints are often defined per slot: the number of UAVs at the vertiport (whether landed, taking off, or landing) must not exceed capacity. Since turnaround takes 1 slot, the UAV is at the vertiport for at least 1 slot after arrival. Then it may leave only after turnaround and any charge. So we need to ensure that for each slot, the number of UAVs at a vertiport <= pad capacity (default 2, except VP_02 at slot 5 is 1). So we must check occupancy.\n\nAlso, charger capacity: each vertiport has 2 chargers. Charging after service flight uses charger slots. Charge_blocks is defined per mission: number of post-service charge blocks (0 to max_charge_blocks=3). Each block takes 1 slot? Typically, charge_blocks indicates how many blocks to charge after turnaround, each block takes some slot? The instance description: \"charge_kwh_per_block\" per UAV, and the charging occurs at destination after turnaround. The charge blocks occupy charger slots for each block's duration? Usually, each charge block occupies one slot of a charger. We need to ensure that at each slot, the number of charging UAVs at a vertiport does not exceed charger capacity (2). The charging occurs for a mission after its turnaround, for the number of charge_blocks slots. So if charge_blocks=2, then for the two slots after turnaround, the UAV occupies a charger.\n\nNow, the baseline decisions: \n- MISSION_01: UAV_02, held false, path PATH_09, depart_slot 6, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_02: UAV_01, held false, path PATH_07, depart 24, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_03: UAV_03, held false, path PATH_08, depart 6, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_04: UAV_01, held false, path PATH_05, depart 20, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_05: UAV_02, held false, path PATH_10, depart 3, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_06: UAV_03, held false, path PATH_06, depart 14, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_07: UAV_03, held false, path PATH_01, depart 3, charge_blocks 0.\n\nCheck these against overrides and constraints:\n\nUAV_02: Initial SOC original 2.750553, but override sets to 1.659556. UAV_02 start vertiport VP_05. MISSION_05 (seq 0) for UAV_02: origin VP_05, destination VP_02, due slot 5, release 3. Path PATH_10 uses COR_08 (travel slots 2). COR_08 closed slots 3-26. Departure slot 3: slots 3 and 4, both closed (3-4 closed) → invalid. So baseline MISSION_05 can't use PATH_10 at depart 3. So that's a conflict.\n\nMISSION_01 (seq 1) for UAV_02: origin VP_02, destination VP_01, due slot 21, release 6. Path PATH_09 uses COR_05 (travel slots 1, open all slots). Depart 6. So that's fine if UAV_02 can get to VP_02 by slot 6 with enough energy, and pad capacity at VP_02 at slot 6? VP_02 pad capacity default 2, but slot 5 has override cap 1. No other override for VP_02. So at slot 6, pad capacity is 2. But does MISSION_05 arrive at VP_02 at some slot? Let's compute if we change MISSION_05. If MISSION_05 uses another path, say PATH_02 (COR_11, travel slots 2, open) or PATH_03 (COR_06, travel slots 3). Currently PATH_10 invalid. So we need to fix MISSION_05 first.\n\nMISSION_05: release 3, due 5. So it must depart >=3 and arrival at destination slot = depart + travel -1? The travel slots indicate duration. Arrival at end of travel? Usually, if depart slot is s, and travel_slots = t, then the flight occupies slots s, s+1, ..., s+t-1, and the arrival slot is s+t-1? Or does it land at s+t? Typically, travel slots includes the departure slot as the first slot. The arrival is at the end of that period. For example, in baseline, MISSION_05 depart 3, travel 2, arrival likely slot 4 (since 3 and 4 are travel slots, arrival at slot 4 end). Then turnaround takes 1 slot: slot 5? So the UAV is at VP_02 at slots 4 (arrival) and 5 (turnaround). Then if charge_blocks=0, it's free at slot 6. So that would put UAV_02 at VP_02 at slots 4 and 5. Pad capacity at VP_02 slot 5 is 1, so only one UAV can be there. UAV_02 alone is fine. But if MISSION_05 uses PATH_02 (travel 2), same timing (depart 3, arrival 4, turnaround 5). If PATH_03 (travel 3), depart 3, arrival 5, turnaround 6. So pad occupancy at slot 5 would be different.\n\nNow check UAV_03: MISSION_07 seq 0: UAV_03, start VP_05, destination VP_02, due slot 26, release 3. Path PATH_01 uses COR_09 (travel slots 2). Depart 3, arrival slot 4? Then turnaround slot 5. So UAV_03 would be at VP_02 at slots 4 (arrival) and 5 (turnaround). This conflicts with UAV_02 if it also is at VP_02 at slot 5? Pad capacity at VP_02 slot 5 is 1 (override). So there could be a conflict if both UAV_02 and UAV_03 are at VP_02 at slot 5. In baseline, MISSION_05 depart 3 (invalid), but if we fix MISSION_05 with PATH_02 (travel 2), arrival 4, turnaround 5, then both UAV_02 and UAV_03 would be at VP_02 at slot 5 → capacity exceeded. If MISSION_05 uses PATH_03 (travel 3), depart 3, arrival 5, turnaround 6, then it avoids slot 5 at VP_02 (arrives at 5, but arrival slot is 5? Travel slots 3 covers slots 3,4,5? Actually, if depart 3, travel 3, the flight occupies slots 3,4,5, and arrival is at the end of slot 5? Then turnaround would be slot 6. In that case, during slot 5, the UAV is in flight (maybe not at VP_02)? But pad occupancy applies when at vertiport only. During flight, it doesn't use pad. So at slot 5, UAV_02 is still in the air, arriving at VP_02 at the end of slot 5, so it doesn't occupy pad at slot 5; it would occupy pad at slots 6 (arrival and turnaround? turnaround at slot 6). So UAV_02 avoids slot 5 pad. Then UAV_03 with PATH_01 (travel 2) arrives at end of slot 4, turnaround at slot 5, so UAV_03 occupies pad at slot 5. That's only one UAV, so fine. So to avoid conflicts, MISSION_05 could use PATH_03, arriving later. But due date is slot 5, so must arrive by slot 5. PATH_03 travel 3, earliest depart 3 gives arrival slot 5? Arrival at end of slot 5, so slot 5 is the arrival slot. Is that considered on-time? Due slot 5, an arrival at slot 5 is likely considered on-time? Usually due slot is the latest allowed arrival slot; arrival at slot 5 is acceptable. So MISSION_05 can use PATH_03 (COR_06, travel 3). COR_06 is open all slots? No closures for COR_06. So depart 3 is possible. Then arrival at 5, turnaround 6, leaving pad occupancy at VP_02 at slots 5? Actually, arrival at slot 5 uses pad? During the arrival slot, the UAV is landing and occupying pad. So at slot 5, UAV_02 would be landing at VP_02. So that would occupy pad at slot 5. UAV_03 lands at slot 4 end, then turnaround at slot 5, so UAV_03 occupies pad at slot 5 as well. That would be 2 UAVs at VP_02 at slot 5, but pad capacity override is 1. So conflict again. So any arrival that happens at slot 5 would conflict with UAV_03's turnaround at slot 5. To avoid, MISSION_05 could depart later (but release is 3, and due is 5, so cannot depart later than 3 because travel time at least 2? Travel times: PATH_02 2 slots, PATH_03 3 slots, PATH_10 2 slots. Early departure is mandatory to meet due 5. So for MISSION_05, travel 2 would arrive at 4 (if depart 3), turnaround 5 (pad 5). Travel 3 would arrive at 5 (pad 5), turnaround 6 (pad 6). So in both cases, pad at VP_02 slot 5 is used (if travel 2: turnaround 5; if travel 3: arrival 5). So UAV_02 will be at VP_02 at slot 5 regardless, unless it charges? No, turnaround is mandatory 1 slot after arrival, so it is at vertiport. So to avoid conflict, we must shift UAV_03's occupancy at VP_02 slot 5. Can we adjust UAV_03 MISSION_07? MISSION_07 seq 0 for UAV_03, due 26, release 3. It uses PATH_01 (COR_09, travel 2) or maybe other paths? Only PATH_01 is listed as path option for MISSION_07. Check: MISSION_07 path_options has only PATH_01. So only that path. So UAV_03 must depart from VP_05 to VP_02 via COR_09, travel 2. Could it depart later than 3? Release is 3, so can depart 3 or any later slot, as long as arrival <= due 26. Depart slot 4 would give arrival 5, turnaround 6, avoiding slot 5 pad? Actually, arrival at end of slot 5 (travel slots 4 and 5? If depart 4, travel 2: slots 4 and 5, arrival end of slot 5, then turnaround slot 6. So at slot 5, UAV_03 is still in flight, not at VP_02. So pad at VP_02 slot 5 is free of UAV_03. So if we delay MISSION_07 depart to 4, then it doesn't occupy VP_02 at slot 5. Then UAV_02 can use either PATH_02 (depart 3, arrival 4, turnaround 5, pad 4 and 5) or PATH_03 (depart 3, arrival 5, turnaround 6, pad 5 and 6). With MISSION_07 depart 4, no UAV_03 at VP_02 slot 5, so pad capacity 1 is fine with one UAV. But we must check pad capacity at slot 4: MISSION_07 depart 4 from VP_05, not at VP_02. UAV_02 arrives at VP_02 at slot 4 if using PATH_02, so pad VP_02 slot 4 has UAV_02 (arrival and maybe turnaround starts? Arrival at end of slot 4, turnaround at slot 5). So slot 4: UAV_02 occupies pad (landing). Pad capacity at VP_02 slot 4 is default 2, so fine. So delaying MISSION_07 to depart 4 resolves the slot 5 conflict.\n\nBut we need to check energy constraints for UAV_02: initial SOC 1.659556 kWh. MISSION_05 must have enough energy for incoming deadhead (none, since start at VP_05), service flight energy. Reserve check after service flight: SOC after flight must be >= reserve (1.5 kWh) after deducting incoming deadhead (none) and service flight energy. So pre-flight SOC minus service energy >= reserve. So required pre-flight SOC >= service energy + reserve. MISSION_05 path options: \n- PATH_02: energy 0.105768 kWh, \n- PATH_03: 0.119328 kWh,\n- PATH_10: 0.156537 kWh.\nSo minimum required initial SOC is max of those? PATH_02: 0.105768+1.5=1.605768. UAV_02 initial 1.659556, so enough for PATH_02 and PATH_03. Reserve is 1.5, so after flight, remaining must be at least 1.5. Actually, reserve check is immediate: after deducting incoming deadhead (0) and service energy, SOC must be >= reserve. So after service, SOC = initial - service_energy >= reserve. Check: 1.659556 - 0.105768 = 1.553788 > 1.5 (PATH_02). For PATH_03: 1.659556 - 0.119328 = 1.540228 > 1.5. So both feasible energy-wise.\n\nBut we must also consider that MISSION_05 is urgent (priority: urgent). Lexicographic objective: first minimize urgent holds. So we should avoid holding urgent missions if possible.\n\nNow, MISSION_01 for UAV_02, seq 1, from VP_02 to VP_01. Path PATH_09 energy 0.094016. Depart 6: arrival slot 6 (travel 1). Turnaround at VP_01 slot 7? Then charge_blocks 0. So we need UAV_02 to have enough SOC after MISSION_05 and any charge to perform MISSION_01. After MISSION_05 and turnaround, UAV_02 may charge at VP_02 if needed. charge_blocks allowed up to 3. UAV_02 can charge at destination VP_02 after MISSION_05. The charge amount per block: 1.2 kWh. So if it charges, it can add energy. After MISSION_05, SOC after service = initial - MISSION_05 energy. Then after turnaround, it can charge. Then for MISSION_01, it needs to cover deadhead? No incoming deadhead listed (incoming_deadhead: null). So just service energy plus reserve check. Required pre-flight SOC for MISSION_01 >= service energy + reserve: 0.094016 + 1.5 = 1.594016. Post MISSION_05 SOC after flight (if PATH_02): 1.553788. Slightly below required. If PATH_03: 1.540228, also below. So UAV_02 needs to charge after MISSION_05 to have enough for MISSION_01. Even with charge_blocks=1, add 1.2 -> SOC becomes 2.753788, sufficient. So we can set charge_blocks=1 or more to meet SOC requirement. Also need to check battery capacity of 16.0, after charge, SOC must not exceed capacity. 2.75 < 16, fine.\n\nBut we must also consider that charging occupies charger slots at VP_02 and takes time. After turnaround, charge_blocks number of slots are used. That might delay departure of MISSION_01. MISSION_01 release slot 6, due 21. If we charge 1 block after MISSION_05, that takes slot ? MISSION_05 arrival timing: if we use PATH_02: depart 3, arrival 4, turnaround 5. After turnaround, charge slots: if charge_blocks=1, it charges during slot 6? Typically, the charging occurs after turnaround, starting at the next slot. So charge_blocks slots: first charge slot is turnaround_slot+1. So with PATH_02 and charge_blocks=1, charge slot = 6. Then MISSION_01 could depart at slot 7 earliest (after charge). But release is 6, so depart 6 is earliest allowed. Is it allowed to start MISSION_01 at depart 6 while the UAV is still charging? The normative order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So post-service charge occurs after turnaround, before next mission's incoming deadhead. So for the next mission (MISSION_01), its incoming deadhead is after the post-service charge. So if we need to depart at slot 6, but charging occupies slot 6, we'd have to delay departure to slot 7. That's allowed (release 6, so depart 7 is >=6, okay). So we can set depart for MISSION_01 to 7. That's later than baseline 6 but still within due 21. So we should adjust depart.\n\nAlternatively, use PATH_03: depart 3, arrival 5, turnaround 6. charge_blocks=1: charge slot 7. Then MISSION_01 could depart at slot 8. Even later. So PATH_02 is better for earlier departure. So use PATH_02 for MISSION_05, charge_blocks=1, depart 3, then MISSION_01 depart 7, path PATH_09.\n\nNow, check pad capacity at VP_02 for these timings: MISSION_05 (PATH_02, depart 3, arrival 4, turnaround 5) → UAV_02 at VP_02 slots 4,5. MISSION_07 (we said depart 4 to avoid conflict): PATH_01, depart 4, travel 2: slots 4,5 (flight), arrival end of 5, turnaround 6. So UAV_03 occupies VP_02 at slot 6 (arrival and turnaround). So no conflict at slot 5. At slot 4, UAV_02 landing, UAV_03 in flight? Not at VP_02, so pad OK. So good.\n\nNow energy for UAV_03: initial SOC 10.678505 kWh, battery capacity 22, reserve 2.5. MISSION_07 energy: PATH_01 0.178505 kWh. After service: 10.678505 - 0.178505 = 10.500000. reserve check >=2.5, ok. Then MISSION_03 seq 1 for UAV_03: origin VP_02 to VP_06, due 9, release 6, urgent. Paths: PATH_04 (travel 4, energy 0.334495), PATH_08 (travel 2, energy 0.167248), PATH_11 (travel 1, energy 0.167248). But PATH_08 uses COR_02 closed 6-29, so can't use that at depart 6 or later (since depart and +1 slots would be 6,7 closed). Could use earlier? Release 6, so earliest depart 6. So PATH_08 would require slots 6,7, both closed. So PATH_08 invalid. PATH_11 uses COR_10, travel 1. COR_10 open? No closure, so it's fine. PATH_04 uses COR_04 (travel 4), open. So feasible paths: PATH_11 (travel 1, energy 0.167248) or PATH_04 (travel 4, energy 0.334495). MISSION_03 due 9, so arrival must be <=9. PATH_11 depart 6, arrival 6 (travel 1), turnaround 7, okay. PATH_04 depart 6, arrival 9 (travel 4 slots 6,7,8,9), arrival 9, turnaround 10, okay but later turnaround. Urgent, so better to finish early to avoid tardiness? Lexicographic: weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait then energy. Minimize tardiness. Due 9, both arrive by 9, so no tardiness if due=9 means arrival at 9 is acceptable. Weighted tardiness: maybe 0 if on time. So both fine. But we also have MISSION_06 seq 2 for UAV_03, origin VP_06, destination VP_03, due 23, release 14. PATH_06 (travel 1, energy 0.50585). So after MISSION_03, UAV_03 is at VP_06, needs to go to VP_03. MISSION_06 release 14, so cannot depart before 14. So after MISSION_03 arrival and turnaround, UAV_03 will wait at VP_06 until release 14. That wait may include charging. So we need to ensure energy for MISSION_06 (including reserve). Also consider charger capacity. So MISSION_03 could use PATH_11 for earlier arrival at VP_06 (arrival 6), then turn around at 7, then idle/charge until 14. That's fine. Or use PATH_04 arrive 9, turnaround 10, then wait. That's also fine. Probably PATH_11 is better to minimize delay plus charge wait? Not sure. Energy consumption lower for PATH_11. So I'll use PATH_11, energy 0.167248.\n\nNow compute SOC after MISSION_07: 10.5. Then before MISSION_03, UAV_03 at VP_02 may have charged after MISSION_07 turnaround. MISSION_07: depart 4, arrival 5, turnaround 6. So after turnaround, it can charge at VP_02 before MISSION_03. MISSION_03 earliest depart could be 7 if charging? release 6, but we need to consider if charging blocks needed. After MISSION_07, SOC=10.5. For MISSION_03, required pre-flight SOC >= service_energy + reserve (2.5) = 0.167248 + 2.5 = 2.667248. So plenty, no charge needed. But we might want to charge to top off for later. To minimize energy usage? Lexicographic last objective is energy, so minimizing total energy used across all missions? That means we might want to charge as little as possible to avoid wasting energy? Actually energy objective probably means minimize total energy consumed (sum of flight energy). Charging doesn't affect flight energy directly, but charging adds to SOC for future flights, but the flight energy is fixed per path. So energy objective is just the sum of the energy_kwh of the chosen paths. So we prefer lower energy paths. So PATH_11 is 0.167248 vs 0.334495. So select PATH_11. Also, no need to charge after MISSION_07 because energy is plenty. So charge_blocks=0 after MISSION_07. However, we may need to charge later for MISSION_06? MISSION_06 needs energy 0.50585 + reserve 2.5 = 3.00585. After MISSION_03, UAV_03 will be at VP_06. Let's compute SOC after MISSION_03: start SOC 10.5 (assuming no charging after MISSION_07 and MISSION_03 start with that SOC). MISSION_03 uses 0.167248, so SOC after service flight = 10.5 - 0.167248 = 10.332752. That's well above 3.00585, so no charge needed for MISSION_06. But MISSION_06 release 14, so it might wait many slots. It could charge to reach higher SOC, but no need. However, we might need to consider battery capacity when charging: 22 kWh max. It already has 10.33, so charging up to 22 would take many blocks (each 0.95 kWh, difference ~11.67 kWh, about 12-13 blocks, max_charge_blocks=3, so can't fully charge anyway). But no urgency. So charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_03 and MISSION_06.\n\nBut we also have UAV_01: MISSION_04 seq 0, origin VP_02, destination VP_07, due 27, release 20, priority routine. Path PATH_05 (travel 2, energy 0.12543). MISSION_02 seq 1, origin VP_07, destination VP_04, due 30, release 24, priority routine, path PATH_07 (travel 1, energy 0.097835). UAV_01 initial SOC 7.5, reserve 1.5, start VP_02. No incoming deadhead for either. So MISSION_04 from VP_02, must have SOC >= 0.12543+1.5=1.62543, easily met. After MISSION_04, SOC = 7.5 - 0.12543 = 7.37457. Then at VP_07, after turnaround (1 slot), it can charge if needed for MISSION_02. MISSION_02 needs 0.097835+1.5=1.597835, so already enough. So no charging needed. But we